    "title": "Pope Leo XIV to announce successor for NYC cardinal - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Eileen Lehpamer ,\n1 hours ago\nEditor’s note: The video above aired in a precious newscast.\nMIDTOWN MANHATTAN (PIX11) — Pope Leo XIV is expected to announce Thursday that he has accepted Timothy Cardinal Dolan’s request for retirement and chosen a successor.\nDolan, who has been the head of the New York Archdiocese since 2009, submitted his resignation in February upon turning 75. That is required under church law.\nMore Local News Pope Francis did not move to replace Dolan before his death in April of 2025. Sources say Pope Leo will make the announcement from the Vatican on Thursday that he has accepted Dolan’s retirement.\nChurch insiders say the Pope will name Bishop Ronald Hicks of the Diocese of Joliet, Illinois, as the next Archbishop of New York.\nMichael E. Lee, who is a professor of theology and the director of the Center for Catholic American Studies at Fordham University, told PIX 11 News, “You had time now for Pope Leo to enter into the office, and get a handle on things, and it also means the transition would be smoother.”\nThe New York Archdiocese announced this week it would sell off land under the Palace Hotel as it agreed to create a $300-million fund to compensate victims of clergy sex abuse.\nProfessor Lee said the Pope likely waited for Cardinal Dolan to make that announcement “just to bring resolution and closure, you’d want that to allow the next archbishop to have a clean slate, to begin his ministry and begin his future.”\nA spokesperson for the New York Archdiocese did not return emails or calls to his cell phone requesting comment.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to PIX11.",
